  "format": "anatomy",
  "concept_key": "selection.relative-strength.rs-line",
  "title": "The RS line leads price",
  "hook": "The market fell 56%. Netflix rose 66%.",
  "takeaway": "Relative strength is a stock's price divided by the market. In the 2008 crash, Netflix fell too, but it rebounded much faster than the market: its RS line hit a new 52-week high in December 2008 while its price was still 35% below its high. Fourteen weeks later the price broke out, and it rose 176% in the next 15 months. A rising RS line flags a possible leader early; it's a watchlist clue, not a buy signal.",
  "learn": [
    "Relative strength is a stock's price divided by the market (here the S&P 500); the line rises when the stock beats the market.",
    "In December 2008, Netflix's RS line hit a new 52-week high while its price was still 35% below its high.",
    "Fourteen weeks later the price broke to a new high, and Netflix rose 176% over the next 15 months while the S&P 500 rose 36%.",
    "A new RS high before a price high is a clue to watch the stock, not a signal to buy it."
  ],
  "glossary": {
    "relative strength": "price divided by the S&P 500"
  },
  "question": null,
  "emotion": "the jolt of seeing a stock hold up while everything else burns, then the satisfaction of having a line that spotted it early",
